<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Loss and Scoring Weights</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A3.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ C.3 Normality-Reference Control ‣ Appendix C Dataset-Wise Ablation Results ‣ LEARN-TS: LLM-Enhanced Alignment and Reconstruction with Normality Guidance for Multivariate Time-Series Anomaly Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> reports sensitivity to the
full-reconstruction weight <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{full}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>full</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{full}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the
normality-alignment weight <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{norm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>norm</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{norm}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while
Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix D Dataset-Wise Sensitivity Analysis ‣ LEARN-TS: LLM-Enhanced Alignment and Reconstruction with Normality Guidance for Multivariate Time-Series Anomaly Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>(a) reports
sensitivity to the discrepancy-gating coefficient
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{gate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>gate</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{gate}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The main experiments use <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{full}}=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>full</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{full}}=0.5</annotation></semantics></math> for all datasets,
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{norm}}=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>norm</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{norm}}=0.02</annotation></semantics></math> for SWaT and <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> for SMD, PSM, and
MSL, and
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{gate}}\in\{1.0,0.05,0.1,0.05\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>gate</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{gate}}\in\{1.0,0.05,0.1,0.05\}</annotation></semantics></math> for SWaT, SMD, PSM,
and MSL, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SWaT is particularly sensitive to removing the full-window reconstruction
objective, normality alignment, or discrepancy-guided modulation, with
pronounced decreases in A-PR and V-PR when the corresponding weight is set
to zero.
Performance is generally more stable across positive weighting values,
although the preferred operating range remains dataset dependent.
PSM is comparatively insensitive to <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{gate}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>gate</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{gate}}</annotation></semantics></math> in A-PR
and V-PR, whereas SMD and MSL exhibit clearer trade-offs between ranking and
threshold-dependent event metrics as the gate strength increases.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Training Masking</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main configuration masks one of the eight temporal patches during each
training forward pass.
Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Sensitivity analysis. ‣ 4.3 Model Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ LEARN-TS: LLM-Enhanced Alignment and Reconstruction with Normality Guidance for Multivariate Time-Series Anomaly Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(d) presents the corresponding four-dataset
macro trend, while
Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Appendix D Dataset-Wise Sensitivity Analysis ‣ LEARN-TS: LLM-Enhanced Alignment and Reconstruction with Normality Guidance for Multivariate Time-Series Anomaly Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>(b) provides the
complete dataset-wise results.
Masking additional patches generally degrades macro performance, with the
strongest effect observed on SWaT.
Although SMD and MSL exhibit small metric-dependent improvements under
heavier masking, these gains are not consistent across datasets or metrics.
Single-patch masking therefore provides the most balanced overall behavior
while matching the exhaustive single-patch masking scheme used at inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Language-Backbone Robustness and Efficiency</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate whether LEARN-TS remains effective across different frozen
language backbones while comparing their computational costs.
Table <a href="#A5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix E Language-Backbone Robustness and Efficiency ‣ LEARN-TS: LLM-Enhanced Alignment and Reconstruction with Normality Guidance for Multivariate Time-Series Anomaly Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> reports efficiency and anomaly detection
performance across all four datasets for GPT-2,
Qwen2.5-1.5B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and
Llama-3.2-1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meta, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#A5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix E Language-Backbone Robustness and Efficiency ‣ LEARN-TS: LLM-Enhanced Alignment and Reconstruction with Normality Guidance for Multivariate Time-Series Anomaly Detection" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, the three frozen
language backbones yield broadly comparable ranking performance
across all four datasets. GPT-2 achieves the highest embedding and
cached scoring throughput throughout, while detection performance
varies by dataset and metric. No backbone consistently outperforms
the others, and larger backbones do not yield uniform gains.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All efficiency measurements were performed on an NVIDIA L40S
GPU with 48 GB of GPU memory.
For SMD, detection metrics are macro-averaged across 28 machines
within each seed. Parameter counts report the range across machines,
peak training GPU memory reports the maximum, and scoring throughput
is computed as total windows divided by total scoring time.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T13" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 13: </span>Backbone efficiency and detection performance on four
datasets. Detection results are mean <math id="A5.T13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> sample standard
deviation over five seeds. Embedding throughput measures prompt
encoding; scoring throughput excludes text encoding and uses
cached embeddings. Bold indicates the best result within each
dataset and column.</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
